Behavioral indicators at MOB 6 begin to deteriorate from the 2022 relaxed cohorts onward. This is the early-warning layer accounts are flagged before they become delinquent.

3. Diagnosing Root Causes
Root causes of portfolio deterioration generally fall into four buckets:
1. Origination quality degradation. Credit policy was loosened - lower score cutoffs, higher DTI tolerances, relaxed income verification. The 2023-Q3 vintage being worse at every MOB (not just recent months) indicates this.
2. Macroeconomic stress. Rate increases, unemployment rises, or sector-specific shocks (e.g., tech layoffs hitting a geographically concentrated portfolio). Macro stress affects all vintages simultaneously, not just recent ones this distinguishes it from origination quality issues.
3. Product or channel mix shift. A channel that grew aggressively (e.g., digital partner channels, buy-now-pay-later) carries different risk characteristics than the seasoned core book. Even without policy changes, shift in mix changes portfolio expected loss.
4. Behavioral / operational factors. Collections capacity constraints, contact rate decline, or customer service friction can worsen self-cure rates without any change in underlying credit quality.
A useful diagnostic is a shift-share decomposition. The objective here is decision-oriented triage: determine whether the portfolio NPL change is primarily coming from composition change (who we booked) or within-segment rate change (how each segment performed).
If the deterioration is mix-dominant, the first response is usually origination policy (cutoffs, overlays, channel controls). If deterioration is rate-dominant, we need a deeper investigation of within-segment drivers (behavioral stress, underwriting drift, servicing/collections execution, and macro sensitivity).
Decompose the change in NPL ratio between two periods into:
\[\Delta \text{NPL} = \underbrace{\Delta \text{Mix}}_{\text{composition effect}} + \underbrace{\Delta \text{Rate within segment}}_{\text{performance effect}} + \underbrace{\text{Interaction}}_{\text{cross effect}}\]
This is not an ad hoc heuristic; it is a standard shift-share / Kitagawa-style decomposition. The starting point is an accounting identity:
\[\text{NPL}_t = \sum_s w_{s,t} r_{s,t}\]
where \(w_{s,t}\) is the share of segment \(s\) in period \(t\) and \(r_{s,t}\) is that segment’s NPL ratio. Once the portfolio NPL is written as a weighted average of segment-level NPL rates, the change from period 1 to period 2 can be decomposed exactly by adding and subtracting the appropriate cross terms:
\[
\Delta \text{NPL} = \sum_s (w_{s,2} - w_{s,1}) r_{s,1}
+ \sum_s w_{s,1}(r_{s,2} - r_{s,1})
+ \sum_s (w_{s,2} - w_{s,1})(r_{s,2} - r_{s,1})
\]
The interpretation is straightforward:

Mix effect: what would have happened if only the segment weights changed, holding each segment’s NPL ratio at its period-1 level?
Rate effect: what would have happened if only segment NPL rates changed, holding portfolio composition fixed at period 1?
Interaction: the joint effect of weights and rates moving at the same time.

That is why the decomposition works: it is an exact breakdown of the change in a weighted average, not a model-based approximation.

# Build segment × cohort NPL panel from account-level data
npl_panel &lt;- accounts |&gt;
  filter(origination_quarter %in% c("2022-Q1", "2022-Q2", "2022-Q3", "2022-Q4",
                                     "2023-Q1", "2023-Q2", "2023-Q3", "2023-Q4")) |&gt;
  group_by(origination_quarter, channel) |&gt;
  summarise(n = n(), n_default = sum(default_12m), .groups = "drop") |&gt;
  mutate(npl_rate = n_default / n)

# Shift-share: compare 2022-Q1 (period 1) vs 2023-Q3 (period 2)
t1_label &lt;- "2022-Q1"
t2_label &lt;- "2023-Q3"

make_decomp &lt;- function(panel, t1, t2) {
  # Extract channel-level shares and NPL rates for both periods
  p1 &lt;- panel |&gt; filter(origination_quarter == t1) |&gt;
    mutate(total_t1 = sum(n), share = n / sum(n)) |&gt;
    select(channel, share_t1 = share, npl_t1 = npl_rate)

  p2 &lt;- panel |&gt; filter(origination_quarter == t2) |&gt;
    mutate(total_t2 = sum(n), share = n / sum(n)) |&gt;
    select(channel, share_t2 = share, npl_t2 = npl_rate)

  # Join and compute effects
  inner_join(p1, p2, by = "channel") |&gt;
    mutate(
      mix_effect  = (share_t2 - share_t1) * npl_t1,
      rate_effect = share_t1 * (npl_t2 - npl_t1),
      interaction = (share_t2 - share_t1) * (npl_t2 - npl_t1)
    ) |&gt;
    summarise(
      portfolio_npl_t1 = sum(share_t1 * npl_t1),
      portfolio_npl_t2 = sum(share_t2 * npl_t2),
      total_change     = portfolio_npl_t2 - portfolio_npl_t1,
      mix_effect       = sum(mix_effect),
      rate_effect      = sum(rate_effect),
      interaction      = sum(interaction)
    )
}

decomp_result &lt;- make_decomp(npl_panel, t1_label, t2_label)
print(decomp_result |&gt; mutate(across(everything(), \(x) round(x, 4))))

